<h2>Documenting a tornado or windstorm claim</h2>
<p>Wind claims after a tornado or straight-line winds can be complicated because damage is scattered across the roof, walls, outbuildings, and contents. A few habits make a real difference:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Photograph the whole property before debris removal</td><td>Shows the extent and direction of damage before it is cleared away</td></tr>
<tr><td>List every structure separately</td><td>Barns, sheds, carports, and fences often fall under a separate limit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keep receipts for tarps, board-up, and tree work</td><td>Reasonable emergency repairs are often reimbursable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Save the NWS survey or storm report for your date</td><td>Helps confirm the cause and timing of loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Track living expenses if you cannot stay home</td><td>Additional living expense coverage may apply</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through the full process.</p>

<h2>Permits in Seguin</h2>
<p>The City of Seguin has its own permit office, and its requirements are separate from the City of San Marcos. Ask the City of Seguin what it requires before a roof replacement, structural repair, or rebuild. Properties outside city limits in unincorporated Guadalupe County follow county requirements. Code-required upgrades are paid only to the extent your policy’s ordinance or law coverage allows; our <a href="/local-building-codes/">building codes guide</a> explains how that works.</p>

<h2>How a San Marcos public adjuster helps Seguin property owners</h2>
